swer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497922} {:answer/id #uuid "674daa87-495d-58ab-9af5-9cdc5062e82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9c66a3-67a6-5fd0-9447-e4938b51f47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498094} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a29fc1-da0c-59ab-8e24-af6b4d9da47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} :answer-field/id #uuid "848fedd1-7778-5bd5-81c5-da60ff5ead33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef6c5e9-0b48-5bca-8fc3-2d7acdfe98a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497988} {:answer/id #uuid "726a5dc3-65f5-538d-af5b-3ba8daedca86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} {:answer/id #uuid "83149379-a893-5cb3-ad7b-b43718777042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498104} {:answer/id #uuid "e0da1ffb-345f-5ae1-91cb-7693db413e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e5a0f2-8f10-5531-ae7d-52d463866902" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fdf52f0-09f2-54b2-864b-627c885837ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-336086" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."} :step/id #uuid "297b5ab8-1639-57a8-8dcf-ef5dd67ae845" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae93f70-eae4-559e-a461-fdfe0abd280a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498287} :answer-field/id #uuid "3daf4d3b-396f-535d-ab9e-524aea10fa43" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a35fdcb-73b8-57ed-b501-fbab000af654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498117} :answer-field/id #uuid "766ab29d-e5ba-5a2d-8e7a-62126c5689d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4cb3a8-c477-5885-9483-f1d0949fce53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498323} :answer-field/id #uuid "913d9aeb-35ba-5403-99b5-0421203894a3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c83096-dcf8-5094-a182-51ebd39feed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ef4a65-1363-5281-b2e0-253a6eed11ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f77715c-0903-5f4b-a449-6816c6d9bb06" :question/math-academy-id "q-334465" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=7t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(4t) &={e}^{4t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ad30acf-66b9-5977-ad36-2183bb8c58c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09b9bd8-e653-5eb4-99a5-e24ca1275327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365661} {:answer/id #uuid "06aa85f1-3426-574e-99c1-322400e91930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497814} :answer-field/id #uuid "4302394f-a0de-5281-a556-4c43f2010e3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809ccb92-46bf-593f-aa3e-fab259413c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365663} {:answer/id #uuid "4148c3f6-7cd3-5b8a-8b0b-2b61c22f4d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497945} :answer-field/id #uuid "3baf4fff-2224-5b2e-97a5-8950130f9bc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f33320-09c2-5afb-8ad2-f98194eb8e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0d2d29-ff61-5f5f-b6b0-49c8e2ecf0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sin(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497827} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ad3991-2ac6-5ad3-a913-929c3b64178c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac97a07e-cd49-5c84-a334-f6d1792e26ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365667} {:answer/id #uuid "873e1c15-5d9d-58fe-a62d-ab1be804a1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498111} :answer-field/id #uuid "918694ef-b209-599f-9859-2c7968e720c9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "511649c4-cd9c-5496-8df1-bb8892a20547" :question/math-academy-id "q-334386" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-6\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((6\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(6\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(6\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+6\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ea51f6a-6ea8-5162-ad6a-900689393007" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd46542-9a80-5921-aec9-c4e0eab15d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497935} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c74f4-d2e2-5a08-a0ec-15c44fc1a4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497968} {:answer/id #uuid "9789113b-b31f-54b4-b8fe-4662096868cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498144} {:answer/id #uuid "d57a43c8-dd84-5c2d-96ed-8415ae761576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b347ff5-a360-5553-944a-1fd66d4ece0a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64196c4e-29d7-5962-b257-222a15c4588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498039} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4515ff-f000-5d07-9351-f9cf05460b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498264} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0b0954-c00e-51da-a8ca-5afe64dfb0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498269} {:answer/id #uuid "efe24b44-977f-576a-b20f-de491d1bb43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with its inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498264} :answer-field/id #uuid "69513af4-694f-5ca8-996c-0c33d8fa859d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1b43a8-0bad-584d-8658-946b10a53deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497825} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bcfca-1c79-5dcc-a769-287b903a87de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498327} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2fe107-c83d-541c-ad1f-d9f4feb898de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with its inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498353} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8a0c7f-af95-54f9-89f1-fb7222915fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498327} :answer-field/id #uuid "84273087-2e18-501d-872d-5ad466c8d262" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d656b9e3-27c9-51a6-a70f-c9d56643be83" :question/math-academy-id "q-336108" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-3}} by the transitive property of equality." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "db020f43-44ad-58e9-afc7-d835ef2b9210"}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497795}]}
